/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/jncia-junos.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("jncia-junos", [["jj1",0,1,"A switch has 24 ports, all in the same VLAN, each connected to one PC. How many broadcast domains and collision domains does it create?",["1 broadcast, 1 collision","1 broadcast, 24 collision","24 broadcast, 1 collision","24 broadcast, 24 collision"],1,"Each switch port is its own collision domain, but one VLAN is one broadcast domain. Answers with 24 broadcast domains confuse ports with VLANs.","Objective 1",["A switch gives each port its own collision domain, so 24 connected ports cannot share a single collision domain; that would describe a hub.",null,"All ports are in one VLAN, so they form a single broadcast domain; only a hub would make them one collision domain.","Switch ports do not split broadcast domains; that would take 24 VLANs or 24 router interfaces."]],["jj2",0,1,"Host 10.1.1.10/24 pings 10.2.2.20. Which address does it resolve with ARP first?",["Its default gateway","10.2.2.20","The DNS server","The broadcast address"],0,"The destination is off-subnet, so the host ARPs for its default gateway and sends the frame there. ARPing for 10.2.2.20 would only happen if it were on the local subnet.","Objective 1",[null,"A host only ARPs directly for a destination on its own subnet, and 10.2.2.20 is outside 10.1.1.0/24.","DNS resolves names to IP addresses; the ping already has an IP, and ARP targets the next hop, not the DNS server.","ARP requests are sent to the broadcast MAC, but the address being resolved is the gateway, not a broadcast address."]],["jj3",0,1,"How many usable host addresses does a /26 IPv4 subnet provide?",["30","64","126","62"],3,"A /26 has 64 addresses; minus network and broadcast leaves 62. 64 counts the reserved addresses.","Objective 1",["30 is the usable count for a /27 (32 addresses), not a /26.","64 is the total size of a /26 block; it still includes the network and broadcast addresses, which cannot be assigned.","126 is the usable count for a /25 (128 addresses), twice the size of a /26.",null]],["jj4",0,1,"An engineer needs the subnet that contains host 172.16.45.130/25. What is the network address?",["172.16.45.0","172.16.45.64","172.16.45.128","172.16.44.128"],2,"A /25 splits the last octet into .0 and .128 blocks, and .130 falls in the .128 block. 172.16.45.0 is the other half.","Objective 1",["172.16.45.0/25 covers only .0 to .127, so it cannot contain .130.","The .64 boundary belongs to /26 blocks; a /25 only has blocks starting at .0 and .128.",null,"Changing the third octet to 44 is wrong because a /25 only splits the last octet; the third octet stays 45."]],["jj5",0,1,"Which prefix identifies IPv6 link-local addresses that every IPv6 interface uses?",["2001:db8::/32","fe80::/10","ff00::/8","fc00::/7"],1,"Link-local addresses are fe80::/10. 2000::/3 is global unicast and fc00::/7 is unique local.","Objective 1",["2001:db8::/32 is the documentation prefix, reserved for examples, not a prefix every interface uses.",null,"ff00::/8 is the IPv6 multicast range, not unicast link-local addressing.","fc00::/7 is the unique local range, the IPv6 counterpart of private IPv4, and is not automatically on every interface."]],["jj6",0,1,"How long is an Ethernet MAC address, and what do the first 24 bits usually identify?",["48 bits; the vendor OUI","32 bits; the network","48 bits; the VLAN ID","64 bits; the interface ID"],0,"MAC addresses are 48 bits and the first 24 bits are the organizationally unique identifier. 64 bits is the IPv6 interface ID length, not a MAC.","Objective 1",[null,"32 bits is the length of an IPv4 address, and MAC addresses do not identify networks.","MACs are 48 bits, but the VLAN ID is a separate 12-bit field in the 802.1Q tag, not part of the MAC.","64 bits is the length of an IPv6 interface ID (EUI-64), not an Ethernet MAC address."]],["jj7",0,1,"A switch receives a unicast frame for a destination MAC that is not yet in its table. What does it do?",["Drops the frame","Sends an ARP request","Returns it to the sending port","Floods it within the VLAN"],3,"Unknown unicast frames are flooded within the VLAN until the MAC is learned. Switches do not ARP to forward frames.","Objective 1",["Switches do not drop unknown unicast frames; dropping would break communication until the MAC was learned.","ARP is a Layer 3 host mechanism for mapping IPs to MACs; a switch forwarding a frame does not send ARP requests.","A switch never sends a frame back out the port it arrived on.",null]],["jj8",0,1,"A voice application uses UDP. Why is UDP preferred over TCP for real-time voice?",["It guarantees in-order delivery of packets","It encrypts payloads","It avoids retransmission delay","It uses larger headers"],2,"UDP has no retransmission or handshake, so late packets are not resent and delay stays low. Guaranteed delivery is a TCP feature.","Objective 1",["In-order, guaranteed delivery is a TCP feature; UDP provides neither.","UDP does not encrypt anything; encryption for voice comes from protocols such as SRTP.",null,"UDP's header is 8 bytes, smaller than TCP's 20 bytes, so larger headers is backwards and would not help anyway."]],["jj9",0,1,"Which Junos default forwarding class is reserved for routing protocol and other control traffic?",["best-effort","network-control","assured-forwarding","expedited-forwarding"],1,"network-control carries routing and control protocol traffic. expedited-forwarding is for low-latency user traffic such as voice.","Objective 1",["best-effort is the default class for ordinary user traffic with no special treatment.",null,"assured-forwarding is for user traffic that needs a guaranteed rate with controlled drop, not control protocols.","expedited-forwarding is for low-latency, low-loss user traffic such as voice, not routing protocol traffic."]],["jj10",0,1,"An ingress interface assigns packets to forwarding classes using only the DSCP value already in each header. What type of classifier is this?",["Behavior aggregate","Multifield classifier","Rewrite rule","Scheduler map"],0,"A behavior aggregate classifier reads one CoS field such as DSCP. A multifield classifier uses a firewall filter matching several fields.","Objective 1",[null,"A multifield classifier uses a firewall filter matching several header fields, not just the existing DSCP value.","A rewrite rule sets markings on egress; it does not assign packets to forwarding classes on ingress.","A scheduler map ties forwarding classes to schedulers for queuing; it does not classify packets."]],["jj11",0,1,"Which CoS component changes the DSCP marking on packets as they leave an interface?",["Classifier","Policer","Drop profile","Rewrite rule"],3,"Rewrite rules set markings on egress for the next hop. Classifiers read markings on ingress.","Objective 1",["A classifier reads markings on ingress to choose a forwarding class; it does not change the DSCP value.","A policer limits traffic rate; although it can set loss priority, it is not the component that rewrites DSCP on egress.","A drop profile controls how packets are dropped as a queue fills; it does not change markings.",null]],["jj12",0,1,"What is the compressed form of 2001:0db8:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0001?",["2001:db8:0:1","2001::db8::1","2001:db8::1","2001:0db8::0:1:0"],2,"Leading zeros can be dropped and one run of zero groups replaced with ::. Using :: twice is not allowed.","Objective 1",["Removing a group of zeros without :: is not allowed; 2001:db8:0:1 would be only four groups and a different address.","Using :: twice is invalid because the number of zero groups each one represents would be ambiguous.",null,"This expands to a different address with the 1 in the wrong group; the original ends in ...:0001."]],["jj13",0,1,"Two hosts are in VLAN 10 and VLAN 20 on the same switch. What is needed for them to communicate?",["An 802.1Q trunk port between the two VLANs","A Layer 3 interface routing between them","A larger MAC table","Spanning Tree"],1,"Different VLANs are different broadcast domains, so traffic must be routed (for example by an IRB interface). A trunk only carries VLANs between switches.","Objective 1",["A trunk carries several VLANs between switches but keeps them separate; it does not let VLAN 10 talk to VLAN 20.",null,"MAC table size limits how many addresses a switch learns; it has nothing to do with crossing VLAN boundaries.","Spanning Tree prevents Layer 2 loops; it does not route between VLANs."]],["jj14",0,2,"Which Junos component runs routing protocols and builds the routing table?",["Routing Engine","Packet Forwarding Engine","Switch fabric","Physical Interface Card"],0,"The Routing Engine is the control plane that runs rpd and builds the routing table. The PFE only forwards using the forwarding table.","Objective 2",[null,"The PFE forwards packets using the forwarding table; it does not run routing protocols.","The switch fabric moves packets between line cards; it has no role in running protocols.","A PIC provides physical ports; it does not run routing protocols or build tables."]],["jj15",0,2,"A transit packet enters a router and leaves toward its destination. Which component normally handles it?",["The Routing Engine CPU","The management daemon (mgd) process","The chassis daemon","The Packet Forwarding Engine"],3,"Transit traffic is forwarded by the PFE using its forwarding table without involving the RE. The RE handles only exception traffic.","Objective 2",["The RE handles only exception (host-bound) traffic; transit packets never touch its CPU.","mgd runs the CLI and commits; it has no role in forwarding packets.","chassisd monitors hardware and alarms; it does not forward traffic.",null]],["jj16",0,2,"Which traffic is exception traffic that the PFE passes to the Routing Engine?",["A web session crossing the router","A packet matching the default route","An OSPF hello sent to the router","A frame switched within a VLAN"],2,"Routing protocol packets addressed to the device are host-bound, so they go to the RE. Traffic crossing the router stays in the PFE.","Objective 2",["A web session crossing the router is transit traffic forwarded entirely by the PFE.","Matching the default route is ordinary forwarding; the PFE handles it without the RE.",null,"Switching a frame within a VLAN is data-plane forwarding done by the PFE, not exception traffic."]],["jj17",0,2,"Which daemon handles the CLI and processes configuration commits?",["rpd","mgd","dcd","chassisd"],1,"mgd, the management daemon, runs the CLI and configuration database. rpd is the routing protocol daemon.","Objective 2",["rpd is the routing protocol daemon; it runs OSPF, BGP and similar protocols, not the CLI.",null,"dcd configures interfaces; it does not run the CLI or the commit process.","chassisd monitors chassis hardware and alarms, not the CLI or commits."]],["jj18",0,2,"An engineer changes an interface's MTU and commits. Which daemon applies the change to the interface?",["dcd","rpd","chassisd","snmpd"],0,"dcd, the device control daemon, configures interfaces. chassisd monitors hardware and alarms.","Objective 2",[null,"rpd handles routing protocols and routes, not physical interface settings like MTU.","chassisd monitors hardware components and alarms; interface configuration belongs to dcd.","snmpd answers SNMP polls and sends traps; it does not apply interface configuration."]],["jj19",0,2,"Where does the PFE get the table it uses to forward packets?",["It builds it from ARP only","It runs its own OSPF and BGP processes locally","It reads the candidate config","The RE copies the forwarding table to it"],3,"The RE selects active routes and pushes the forwarding table to the PFE. The PFE does not run routing protocols.","Objective 2",["ARP only maps next-hop IPs to MACs; it cannot tell the PFE which prefixes go where.","The PFE does not run routing protocols; OSPF and BGP run on the RE in rpd.","The candidate config is uncommitted configuration text and is not used to forward packets.",null]],["jj20",0,2,"Why is traffic from the PFE to the Routing Engine rate-limited?",["To save forwarding table space","To speed up transit traffic through the PFE","To protect the control plane from floods","To encrypt management sessions"],2,"Rate limiting host-bound traffic stops a flood from overwhelming the RE. It does not change forwarding table size.","Objective 2",["The size of the forwarding table has nothing to do with how much host-bound traffic reaches the RE.","Transit traffic never goes to the RE, so limiting RE-bound traffic does not speed it up.",null,"Rate limiting controls volume; it does not encrypt anything, which SSH or other protocols handle."]],["jj21",0,2,"A router receives a packet whose TTL reaches zero. How is it handled?",["The PFE forwards it with the TTL reset to 64","It is sent to the RE as exception traffic","It is queued for retransmission","It is mirrored to all ports"],1,"Expired TTL needs an ICMP time-exceeded reply, so the packet is treated as exception traffic. It is never forwarded onward.","Objective 2",["Routers never reset a TTL upward; an expired packet must be dropped and an ICMP time-exceeded sent.",null,"IP routers do not retransmit packets; retransmission is a TCP endpoint function.","Mirroring to all ports is flooding behavior for unknown frames, not how a router treats an expired packet."]],["jj22",0,2,"What is a key benefit of separating the control plane and forwarding plane in Junos?",["Heavy RE load does not stop forwarding","The RE forwards faster","The PFE no longer needs a forwarding table","Interfaces need no configuration"],0,"The PFE keeps forwarding even while the RE is busy with routing or management tasks. The RE is not in the transit path.","Objective 2",[null,"The RE is not in the transit path at all, so separation does not make the RE forward faster.","The PFE absolutely needs a forwarding table; it is the table it uses to forward every packet.","Interfaces still need configuration; the control/forwarding split does not remove that."]],["jj23",0,2,"What is the main difference between Junos OS and Junos OS Evolved?",["Evolved has no CLI","Junos OS cannot route IPv6","Evolved uses a different config syntax","Evolved runs on a Linux base"],3,"Junos OS Evolved runs on Linux, while traditional Junos OS is based on FreeBSD. Both keep the same CLI and configuration style.","Objective 2",["Junos OS Evolved keeps the same CLI as Junos OS.","Traditional Junos OS fully supports IPv6 routing.","Both use the same configuration syntax and commit model; that consistency is a design goal.",null]],["jj24",0,2,"An engineer wants one filter that protects the RE from unwanted SSH attempts on every interface. Where should it be applied?",["As an output filter on ge-0/0/0","On the management interface only","As an input filter on lo0","In the forwarding table"],2,"An input filter on lo0 applies to all host-bound traffic regardless of the arrival interface. Applying it to one interface misses others.","Objective 2",["An output filter on ge-0/0/0 inspects traffic leaving one interface, not SSH arriving at the RE from every interface.","Filtering only the management interface leaves SSH to the router's other interface addresses unprotected.",null,"The forwarding table holds routes, not packet filters; filters are applied to interfaces."]],["jj25",0,2,"Which daemon monitors hardware components and raises chassis alarms?",["mgd (management)","chassisd (chassis)","rpd (routing protocols)","dcd (device control)"],1,"chassisd manages chassis hardware and alarms. mgd handles management and configuration.","Objective 2",["mgd is the management daemon behind the CLI and commits, not hardware monitoring.",null,"rpd runs routing protocols; it does not monitor fans, power or raise chassis alarms.","dcd configures interfaces; chassis hardware monitoring is done by chassisd."]],["jj26",0,2,"Which statement about Junos software across Juniper platforms is correct?",["One OS with the same CLI across families","Each platform family needs its own separate CLI","Switches do not run Junos","Only routers support commit"],0,"Junos uses a common OS and CLI across routing, switching and security products. The commit model is the same everywhere.","Objective 2",[null,"Juniper's routing, switching and security families share one Junos CLI; that is its main selling point.","EX and QFX switches run Junos OS.","The commit model works the same on switches, routers and firewalls, not only routers."]],["jj27",0,2,"Which command shows the routes the RE has pushed to the PFE?",["show route","show interfaces terse","show pfe statistics traffic detail","show route forwarding-table"],3,"`show route forwarding-table` shows the forwarding table. `show route` shows the RE's routing table, including inactive routes.","Objective 2",["`show route` shows the RE's routing table, including inactive routes, not what was pushed to the PFE.","`show interfaces terse` lists interface status and addresses, not routes.","`show pfe statistics traffic` shows PFE packet counters, not the forwarding table contents.",null]],["jj28",0,3,"A prompt reads `admin@R1#`. What mode is the user in?",["Operational mode","Shell mode","Configuration mode","Single-user recovery mode"],2,"The # prompt, usually with an [edit] banner, means configuration mode. Operational mode ends with >.","Objective 3",["Operational mode ends in >, not #.","The shell prompt looks like % (for example root@R1%), not the CLI #.",null,"Single-user recovery mode is a boot option for password recovery, not what the # CLI prompt shows."]],["jj29",0,3,"An engineer in configuration mode wants to check interface status without leaving. Which command works?",["show interfaces terse","run show interfaces terse","exit show interfaces terse","op show interfaces terse"],1,"`run` executes an operational command from configuration mode. `show` in configuration mode shows configuration, not interface status.","Objective 3",["In configuration mode, `show` displays the configuration hierarchy, not interface status.",null,"`exit` leaves the current level or mode; it does not take a command to run.","`op` runs an op script, not an ordinary operational command from configuration mode."]],["jj30",0,3,"Which command shows only the lines of `show interfaces terse` that contain \"ge-0/0\"?",["show interfaces terse | match ge-0/0","show interfaces terse | find ge-0/0","show interfaces terse | count ge-0/0","show interfaces terse | except ge-0/0"],0,"`| match` keeps only matching lines. `| find` starts at the first match but prints everything after it.","Objective 3",[null,"`| find` starts at the first matching line and prints everything after it, including non-matching lines.","`| count` only prints a line count, not the matching lines.","`| except` hides lines that contain the string, the opposite of what is wanted."]],["jj31",0,3,"An engineer wants the whole configuration as one-line commands to paste into another device. Which pipe should be used?",["| display xml","| compare","| no-more","| display set"],3,"`| display set` prints the configuration as set commands. `| no-more` only disables paging.","Objective 3",["`| display xml` outputs the configuration as XML, which cannot be pasted as CLI commands.","`| compare` shows differences between configurations, not the full config.","`| no-more` only turns off paging; the output stays in hierarchical format.",null]],["jj32",0,3,"The user is at [edit protocols ospf area 0.0.0.0] and wants to return to the top of the hierarchy. Which command?",["up","exit configuration-mode","top","quit"],2,"`top` jumps to the top of the hierarchy. `exit configuration-mode` leaves configuration mode entirely.","Objective 3",["`up` moves only one level (or a given number of levels), not straight to the top.","`exit configuration-mode` leaves configuration mode entirely instead of going to the top of the hierarchy.",null,"`quit` exits the current level (like exit), not to the top of the hierarchy."]],["jj33",0,3,"Two admins must edit the same router, and each wants to commit only their own changes. Which command should each use?",["configure exclusive","configure private","configure batch","edit shared"],1,"`configure private` gives each user a private candidate so only their changes commit. Exclusive locks everyone else out.","Objective 3",["`configure exclusive` locks the configuration so the second admin cannot make changes at all.",null,"`configure batch` groups commits from several users into one batch; it does not isolate each user's changes.","`edit shared` is not a Junos configuration mode command; plain `configure` is shared mode."]],["jj34",0,3,"Which configuration is the device running right now?",["The active","The candidate","The rescue","The factory default"],0,"The active configuration is running; candidate changes apply only after commit. The rescue configuration is a saved fallback.","Objective 3",[null,"The candidate holds uncommitted changes and does not take effect until it is committed.","The rescue configuration is a saved known-good fallback, not what is running.","The factory default is only active on a new or zeroized device, not normally what runs."]],["jj35",0,3,"What does pressing Tab do in the Junos CLI that Space does not?",["Runs the command","Shows the full command history list","Leaves the current level","Completes user-defined names"],3,"Tab completes both keywords and user-defined names such as policy names; Space completes only built-in keywords.","Objective 3",["Neither Tab nor Space runs a command; Enter does.","Command history is viewed with `show cli history` or the arrow keys, not Tab.","Leaving a level uses `exit` or `up`; Tab does not change the hierarchy position.",null]],["jj36",0,3,"An engineer needs to count how many interfaces are down. Which command gives a single number?",["show interfaces terse | find down","show interfaces terse | last","show interfaces terse | match down | count","show interfaces terse | except up | no-more"],2,"Filtering with match and then counting gives the number of lines. `| except down` shows the up lines instead.","Objective 3",["`| find down` prints from the first match onward, not a count.","`| last` shows the final lines of the output, not a count of down interfaces.",null,"`| except up` hides lines with \"up\" and would also drop admin-up/link-down lines; `no-more` just disables paging and gives no number."]],["jj37",0,3,"Which command gives a detailed description and syntax for a configuration statement from the CLI?",["help topic","help reference","help history","help syslog messages"],1,"`help reference` shows statement syntax and hierarchy. `help topic` gives background on a feature.","Objective 3",["`help topic` gives general background on a feature, not the syntax of a specific statement.",null,"There is no `help history`; command history is shown with `show cli history`.","`help syslog` explains system log message tags, not configuration statement syntax."]],["jj38",0,3,"A team wants a browser-based GUI to configure a branch SRX. What must be configured?",["system services web-management","system services netconf","system login class","system services extension-service"],0,"J-Web is enabled under `system services web-management` with HTTP or HTTPS. NETCONF is an API, not a GUI.","Objective 3",[null,"NETCONF is an XML API for automation over SSH, not a browser GUI.","Login classes define user permissions; they do not enable a web interface.","`extension-service` enables programmable APIs such as JET, not the J-Web GUI."]],["jj39",0,3,"Before committing, an engineer wants to see exactly what changed from the active config. Which command?",["show | display set","commit check","show system commit","show | compare"],3,"`show | compare` lists additions and deletions against the active configuration. `commit check` only validates syntax and semantics.","Objective 3",["`show | display set` prints the whole candidate as set commands but does not highlight changes.","`commit check` validates syntax and semantics but does not list what changed.","`show system commit` lists commit history, not the differences in the current candidate.",null]],["jj40",0,3,"Which command moves up two levels in the configuration hierarchy?",["exit 2","top 2","up 2","back 2"],2,"`up` accepts a number of levels. `top` always goes to the top of the hierarchy.","Objective 3",["`exit` has no count argument; it leaves one level at a time.","`top` always jumps straight to the top and takes no count.",null,"There is no `back` command in the Junos CLI."]],["jj41",0,4,"A new router with the factory default configuration rejects the first commit. What is the most likely missing setting?",["A host name","A root password","An NTP server","A loopback address"],1,"Junos requires root authentication before the first commit. A host name is useful but not mandatory.","Objective 4",["A host name is recommended but not mandatory; Junos commits without one.",null,"NTP is optional; the router commits without a time server.","A loopback address is good practice but is not required for a commit."]],["jj42",0,4,"An engineer is changing a remote router's management filter and fears losing access. Which command reduces the risk?",["commit confirmed 5","commit check","commit and-quit","commit synchronize"],0,"`commit confirmed 5` rolls back automatically after 5 minutes unless confirmed. `commit check` does not apply anything.","Objective 4",[null,"`commit check` only validates the candidate and applies nothing, so it cannot protect access once the change is applied.","`commit and-quit` commits and leaves configuration mode with no automatic rollback.","`commit synchronize` copies the commit to the backup RE; it offers no rollback timer."]],["jj43",0,4,"How many previous committed configurations does Junos keep for rollback?",["10","20","32","50"],3,"Junos stores rollback 0 through 49, which is 50 configurations. Rollback 0 is the active one.","Objective 4",["10 is too low; Junos keeps rollback numbers up to 49.","20 is too low; Junos keeps rollback 0 through 49.","32 is not the Junos limit; it keeps 50 versions (0 through 49).",null]],["jj44",0,4,"After a bad change was committed, the engineer wants to restore the previous version. Which sequence works?",["rollback 0, then commit","load factory-default, then commit","rollback 1, then commit","rollback 1 only"],2,"`rollback 1` loads the previous version into the candidate and commit makes it active. Without commit nothing changes.","Objective 4",["`rollback 0` loads the current active configuration, which still contains the bad change.","`load factory-default` wipes the whole configuration rather than returning to the previous version.",null,"`rollback 1` only changes the candidate; without commit the bad configuration stays active."]],["jj45",0,4,"In ge-1/2/3.100, what does 100 represent?",["The VLAN ID","The logical unit","The port number","The PIC slot number"],1,"The number after the dot is the logical unit. It may match a VLAN ID but that is a separate setting.","Objective 4",["The VLAN ID is configured separately with vlan-id; the unit number is only a label, even if it often matches.",null,"The port number is 3, the last number before the dot.","The PIC slot is 2, the middle number, not the number after the dot."]],["jj46",0,4,"Where is an IPv4 address configured on a Junos interface?",["Under the unit's family inet","At the physical interface level","Under routing-options","Under system services"],0,"Addresses are set on a logical unit under family inet (for example unit 0 family inet address). Physical properties like MTU sit above the unit.","Objective 4",[null,"The physical interface level holds settings like MTU and speed; IP addresses go on a logical unit.","routing-options holds static routes and router ID, not interface addresses.","system services enables management services such as SSH, not interface addresses."]],["jj47",0,4,"An engineer wants to replace the entire candidate configuration with a file. Which command?",["load merge","load patch","load merge relative","load override"],3,"`load override` discards the candidate and uses the file. `load merge` combines the file with the existing candidate.","Objective 4",["`load merge` combines the file with the existing candidate instead of replacing it.","`load patch` applies a diff-style patch to the candidate, not a full replacement.","`load merge relative` merges statements at the current hierarchy level; it still keeps the rest of the candidate.",null]],["jj48",0,4,"A term should stay in the configuration but be ignored for now. Which command does that?",["delete","annotate","deactivate","insert before"],2,"`deactivate` marks it inactive so it is kept but ignored at commit. `delete` removes it.","Objective 4",["`delete` removes the statement from the configuration entirely.","`annotate` only adds a comment; the statement stays active.",null,"`insert before` reorders terms; it does not disable anything."]],["jj49",0,4,"The same MTU must be set on every ge- interface. Which feature avoids repeating it?",["A rescue configuration","A configuration group with apply-groups","A login class","A separate routing instance per interface"],1,"Groups hold shared statements, and wildcards like <ge-*> apply them to matching interfaces. A rescue configuration is only a backup.","Objective 4",["The rescue configuration is a saved fallback, not a way to share statements across interfaces.",null,"A login class controls user permissions, not interface settings.","Routing instances separate routing tables; they do not apply MTU to interfaces."]],["jj50",0,4,"A new admin needs full configuration rights. Which predefined login class fits?",["super-user","operator","read-only","unauthorized"],0,"super-user has all permissions. operator can clear and restart some things but cannot configure.","Objective 4",[null,"operator can view and clear or restart some things but cannot change the configuration.","read-only can only view information and cannot configure.","unauthorized has no permissions at all."]],["jj51",0,4,"Which command saves the current active configuration as the rescue configuration?",["save rescue","rollback rescue","load rescue","request system configuration rescue save"],3,"`request system configuration rescue save` stores it. `rollback rescue` loads it back into the candidate.","Objective 4",["`save rescue` is not a valid command; `save` writes the configuration to a named file.","`rollback rescue` loads the rescue configuration into the candidate; it does not save one.","`load rescue` is not how Junos saves a rescue configuration; loading brings configuration in, not out.",null]],["jj52",0,4,"An engineer wants a change to take effect at 02:00 during a maintenance window. Which option?",["commit confirmed","commit comment","commit at","commit check"],2,"`commit at` schedules the commit. `commit confirmed` applies now with an automatic rollback.","Objective 4",["`commit confirmed` applies the change now, with an automatic rollback if not confirmed.","`commit comment` records a note in the commit history but still commits immediately.",null,"`commit check` only validates the candidate and applies nothing."]],["jj53",0,4,"Which interface is the out-of-band management Ethernet port on many Junos routers?",["lo0","fxp0","irb","ae10"],1,"fxp0 (em0 or me0 on some platforms) is the management port. lo0 is the loopback.","Objective 4",["lo0 is the loopback interface, a logical interface with no physical port.",null,"irb is the integrated routing and bridging interface used to route between VLANs, not a management port.","ae10 is an aggregated Ethernet (LAG) bundle, not the out-of-band management port."]],["jj54",0,4,"Which command adds a note to a configuration statement that others will see in the config?",["annotate","comment","describe","note"],0,"`annotate` adds a comment above the statement. `commit comment` records a note in the commit history instead.","Objective 4",[null,"There is no `comment` configuration command; comments are added with annotate.","`describe` is not a Junos command; the description statement labels interfaces but is not a general note on any statement.","`note` is not a Junos CLI command."]],["jj55",0,5,"An engineer wants to watch live input and output rates and errors on ge-0/0/1. Which command?",["show log messages","monitor traffic interface ge-0/0/1","show interfaces terse","monitor interface ge-0/0/1"],3,"`monitor interface` shows real-time counters. `monitor traffic interface` captures packets to and from the RE instead.","Objective 5",["`show log messages` shows syslog entries, not live traffic rates and errors.","`monitor traffic interface` captures and decodes host-bound packets rather than showing live counters.","`show interfaces terse` shows status and addresses once, with no live rates or error counters.",null]],["jj56",0,5,"A router seems to be sending OSPF hellos incorrectly. Which command decodes packets to and from the RE on an interface?",["show ospf interface ge-0/0/1 extensive","show ospf neighbor","monitor traffic interface ge-0/0/1","show route"],2,"`monitor traffic interface` works like tcpdump for host-bound traffic. `show interfaces extensive` gives counters, not packet contents.","Objective 5",["`show ospf interface ... extensive` shows OSPF interface state and timers, not decoded packets.","`show ospf neighbor` lists adjacency states; it does not decode hello packets.",null,"`show route` displays the routing table, not packet contents."]],["jj57",0,5,"Where are most system events logged by default?",["/var/tmp/messages","/var/log/messages","/config/juniper.conf","/var/db/log"],1,"The default syslog file is /var/log/messages, read with `show log messages`. /config holds configuration files.","Objective 5",["/var/tmp is a temporary storage directory, commonly used for uploaded software packages, not the default log.",null,"/config/juniper.conf holds the active configuration, not logs.","/var/db holds databases and internal state files, not the default system log."]],["jj58",0,5,"An engineer enabled OSPF traceoptions to debug an adjacency, and the issue is fixed. What should be done next?",["Remove or deactivate the traceoptions","Leave traceoptions running for future issues","Reboot the router","Delete /var/log/messages"],0,"Traceoptions use CPU and disk, so they should be removed after troubleshooting. Deleting the main log loses history.","Objective 5",[null,"Leaving traceoptions running keeps consuming CPU and disk and can fill storage.","Rebooting is unnecessary disruption and does not remove traceoptions from the configuration.","Deleting /var/log/messages destroys useful history and does nothing to stop the tracing."]],["jj59",0,5,"Which command installs a new Junos package and restarts the device to finish?",["request system reboot pkg.tgz","load override /var/tmp/pkg.tgz","request system software validate /var/tmp/pkg.tgz reboot","request system software add /var/tmp/pkg.tgz reboot"],3,"`request system software add ... reboot` installs and restarts. `load override` loads configuration, not software.","Objective 5",["`request system reboot` only restarts the device; it cannot install a package.","`load override` loads a configuration file, not a software package.","`request system software validate` only checks the package against the current configuration without installing it.",null]],["jj60",0,5,"A router's root password is lost. What does recovery require?",["SSH with any user","A factory reset through the J-Web GUI","Console access and recovery mode","An SNMP write community"],2,"Password recovery is done from the console by booting into recovery (single-user) mode. It does not need a factory reset.","Objective 5",["Remote SSH requires a working login, so it cannot bypass a lost root password.","A factory reset is unnecessary and erases the whole configuration; recovery keeps it.",null,"SNMP cannot change the root password, and an SNMP write community would not bypass authentication."]],["jj61",0,5,"A router must be shut down before being moved. Which command stops Junos safely?",["request system zeroize","request system halt","restart routing","clear system commit"],1,"`request system halt` stops the OS gracefully. `zeroize` erases the configuration and data.","Objective 5",["`request system zeroize` erases all configuration and data, which is destructive rather than a safe shutdown.",null,"`restart routing` only restarts rpd; the OS keeps running.","`clear system commit` clears a pending commit, not a shutdown."]],["jj62",0,5,"Disk space is low on a router. Which command removes old log files and temporary files?",["request system storage cleanup","file delete /config/juniper.conf.gz","request system zeroize","clear log all"],0,"`request system storage cleanup` lists and removes unneeded files safely. Deleting /config would destroy saved configurations.","Objective 5",[null,"Deleting /config/juniper.conf.gz destroys the saved configuration and could leave the device unbootable to its config.","`request system zeroize` wipes the whole device, far beyond freeing disk space.","`clear log` truncates a named log file; it does not clean temporary files and `clear log all` is not the storage cleanup tool."]],["jj63",0,5,"Which command shows active hardware alarms such as a failed fan?",["show system processes","show system users","show version","show chassis alarms"],3,"`show chassis alarms` lists chassis alarms. `show version` shows software versions.","Objective 5",["`show system processes` lists running processes, not hardware alarms.","`show system users` shows logged-in users.","`show version` shows software versions and model, not active alarms.",null]],["jj64",0,5,"An engineer wants to check RE CPU and memory usage and temperature. Which command?",["show system uptime","show interfaces extensive","show chassis routing-engine","show system storage partitions"],2,"`show chassis routing-engine` reports RE utilization and temperature. `show system uptime` shows boot and commit times.","Objective 5",["`show system uptime` shows boot, last-commit and current times, not CPU or temperature.","`show interfaces extensive` gives detailed interface counters, not RE utilization.",null,"`show system storage partitions` shows disk partitions, not CPU, memory or temperature."]],["jj65",0,5,"Which configuration sends syslog messages of severity warning and above to a remote server 10.0.0.50?",["set snmp trap-group 10.0.0.50","set system syslog host 10.0.0.50 any warning","set system ntp server 10.0.0.50","set system syslog file remote-10.0.0.50 any warning"],1,"`system syslog host` sends to a remote server. `syslog file` writes a local file.","Objective 5",["SNMP trap groups send SNMP traps, not syslog messages.",null,"`system ntp server` configures time synchronization, not logging.","`syslog file` writes to a local file named remote-10.0.0.50; it does not send to the server."]],["jj66",0,5,"Which configuration keeps a router's clock in sync with 192.0.2.10?",["set system ntp server 192.0.2.10","set system time-zone 192.0.2.10","set system syslog host 192.0.2.10","set routing-options static route 192.0.2.10"],0,"`system ntp server` points the router at an NTP server. Time zone only changes how time is shown.","Objective 5",[null,"Time zone only changes how time is displayed and takes a zone name, not a server address.","`syslog host` sends log messages to that address; it does not synchronize the clock.","A static route to the address only affects reachability, not time synchronization."]],["jj67",0,5,"After an upgrade an engineer must confirm the running software. Which command?",["show system storage","show chassis hardware","show system snapshot","show version"],3,"`show version` lists the running Junos release. `show chassis hardware` shows hardware inventory.","Objective 5",["`show system storage` shows disk usage, not the running software release.","`show chassis hardware` lists hardware inventory and serial numbers, not the Junos version.","`show system snapshot` shows backup snapshot images, which may differ from what is running.",null]],["jj68",0,5,"Traceroute from a router fails after the third hop. What does this suggest?",["The router's local outgoing interface is down","DNS is broken","A problem at or after the fourth hop","The router's clock is wrong"],2,"Replies stop after hop three, so the problem lies at or beyond the next hop (or it blocks ICMP). A down local interface would fail at hop one.","Objective 5",["If the local outgoing interface were down, traceroute would fail at the very first hop.","DNS only affects name resolution of hops; traceroute to an IP still gets replies from each hop.",null,"A wrong clock does not stop routers along the path from replying to traceroute probes."]],["jj69",0,6,"A router has routes to 10.1.0.0/16 and 10.1.1.0/24. Which route is used for traffic to 10.1.1.5?",["10.1.0.0/16","10.1.1.0/24","The one with lower preference","The oldest route"],1,"Longest-prefix match picks the most specific route, the /24. Preference matters only between routes to the same prefix.","Objective 6",["The /16 also matches, but it is less specific; longest-prefix match prefers the /24.",null,"Preference only compares routes to the same prefix; these are different prefixes, so longest match decides.","Route age is not used to choose between different prefixes in Junos."]],["jj70",0,6,"The same prefix is learned from a static route and from OSPF internal. Which becomes active by default?",["Static, preference 5","OSPF, preference 10","Whichever has more hops","Both, load-balanced"],0,"Static routes have preference 5, lower than OSPF internal 10, so static wins. Lower preference is better.","Objective 6",[null,"OSPF internal has preference 10, higher (worse) than static's 5, so it loses.","Hop count is a metric within one protocol, not how Junos chooses between protocols.","Routes from different sources with different preferences are not load-balanced; only the best becomes active."]],["jj71",0,6,"What is the default Junos route preference for BGP routes?",["20","110","200","170"],3,"BGP routes have preference 170 in Junos. 20 and 200 are administrative distances used by another vendor.","Objective 6",["20 is the eBGP administrative distance on another vendor's platform, not the Junos preference.","110 is OSPF's administrative distance on another vendor's platform, not the Junos BGP preference.","200 is the iBGP administrative distance on another vendor's platform, not the Junos preference.",null]],["jj72",0,6,"Which routing table holds IPv4 unicast routes in the master instance?",["inet6.0","inet.3","inet.0","mpls.0"],2,"inet.0 holds IPv4 unicast routes. inet6.0 is for IPv6.","Objective 6",["inet6.0 holds IPv6 unicast routes, not IPv4.","inet.3 holds MPLS LSP next hops used to resolve BGP next hops, not general IPv4 unicast routes.",null,"mpls.0 holds MPLS label-switching entries, not IPv4 unicast routes."]],["jj73",0,6,"In `show route` output, what does an asterisk (*) next to a route mean?",["The route is hidden","It is the active route","It is a static route","It was learned in the last minute"],1,"* marks the active route that is used for forwarding. Hidden routes are shown only with `show route hidden`.","Objective 6",["Hidden routes are not marked with * and appear only with `show route hidden`.",null,"Static routes show [Static/5] in the output; the * marks any active route regardless of source.","Route age is shown as a timer next to the route, not by the asterisk."]],["jj74",0,6,"Which configuration adds an IPv4 default route via 10.0.0.1?",["set routing-options static route 0.0.0.0/0 next-hop 10.0.0.1","set protocols static default 10.0.0.1","set interfaces default-route 10.0.0.1","set routing-options default-gateway 10.0.0.1"],0,"Static routes live under `routing-options static route`. `protocols static` does not exist in Junos.","Objective 6",[null,"`protocols static` does not exist in Junos; static routes are under routing-options.","Interfaces do not have a default-route statement; routes are configured under routing-options.","There is no default-gateway statement under routing-options; a Junos default route is a static route to 0.0.0.0/0."]],["jj75",0,6,"An engineer wants a backup static default route via a second ISP that is used only if the primary fails. What should be configured?",["A second next-hop with the same preference value","A reject next hop","A discard route","A qualified-next-hop with a higher preference"],3,"A qualified next hop with a higher preference stays inactive until the primary is gone. Equal preference would load-balance or pick one.","Objective 6",["Two next hops with the same preference are both usable, so the backup would not stay idle.","A reject next hop drops traffic and sends ICMP unreachable; it cannot forward to the backup ISP.","A discard route drops traffic silently; it cannot forward to the backup ISP.",null]],["jj76",0,6,"What is the difference between `discard` and `reject` static next hops?",["Discard sends ICMP unreachable","Only reject drops traffic","Reject sends ICMP unreachable","Only discard installs a route"],2,"Both drop traffic; reject also sends ICMP unreachable, while discard drops silently.","Objective 6",["It is reject, not discard, that sends ICMP unreachable; discard drops silently.","Both discard and reject drop the traffic; they differ only in whether ICMP is sent.",null,"Both discard and reject install a route in the routing table."]],["jj77",0,6,"A service provider wants separate routing tables for two customers on one router without MPLS VPN signaling. Which instance type fits?",["forwarding","virtual-router","vrf with route targets","l2vpn"],1,"A virtual-router instance gives an independent routing table without VPN signaling. vrf is used for Layer 3 VPNs.","Objective 6",["A forwarding instance is used for filter-based forwarding and does not give a customer its own independent routing table with interfaces.",null,"A vrf instance with route targets is used for MPLS Layer 3 VPNs, which is the signaling the provider wants to avoid.","An l2vpn instance provides Layer 2 VPN services, not separate IPv4 routing tables."]],["jj78",0,6,"A virtual-router instance is named CUST-A. What is the name of its IPv4 routing table?",["CUST-A.inet.0","inet.CUST-A","inet.0.CUST-A","CUST-A.inet6.0"],0,"Instance tables are named <instance>.inet.0. CUST-A.inet6.0 would be the IPv6 table.","Objective 6",[null,"Junos puts the instance name first, not after inet.","The instance name is a prefix, not a suffix after inet.0.","CUST-A.inet6.0 is the instance's IPv6 table, not its IPv4 table."]],["jj79",0,6,"What is the default preference of an OSPF external route in Junos?",["10","100","170","150"],3,"OSPF external routes use 150. 10 is OSPF internal.","Objective 6",["10 is the preference of OSPF internal routes, not external routes.","100 is the default preference for RIP, not OSPF external.","170 is the default preference for BGP, not OSPF external.",null]],["jj80",0,6,"Which protocol type exchanges routes between different autonomous systems?",["IGP such as OSPF","First-hop protocol such as VRRP","EGP such as BGP","Link-layer protocol such as LLDP"],2,"BGP is the exterior gateway protocol used between autonomous systems. OSPF routes within one AS.","Objective 6",["OSPF is an interior gateway protocol that routes within one AS.","VRRP provides a redundant default gateway on a LAN; it does not exchange routes.",null,"LLDP discovers neighbors at Layer 2 and exchanges no routes."]],["jj81",0,6,"A static route stays inactive even though it is configured. What is a common cause?",["Its preference is too low","The next hop is not reachable","It is missing a description statement","The router has no loopback"],1,"Junos needs the next hop to resolve through a directly connected route, or the static route stays inactive. A low preference makes a route more preferred.","Objective 6",["A lower preference makes a route more preferred, so it cannot be what keeps it inactive.",null,"A description is optional and has no effect on whether a route is active.","A loopback is not needed for a static route to become active."]],["jj82",0,7,"Which policy direction controls routes placed into the routing table from a protocol?",["Import","Export","Forwarding","Input filter"],0,"Import policies act between the protocol and the routing table. Export policies control what is advertised out.","Objective 7",[null,"Export policies control routes sent from the routing table into a protocol, not routes placed into the table.","Forwarding table export policies affect load balancing to the PFE, not which protocol routes enter the routing table.","An input filter is a firewall filter acting on packets, not on routes."]],["jj83",0,7,"Static routes are not appearing in OSPF on neighbors. Why, by default?",["OSPF import rejects statics","Static routes have preference 5","OSPF needs BGP","OSPF export rejects all routes"],3,"The OSPF default export policy rejects everything, so an export policy is needed to redistribute statics. Import policies do not affect what is advertised.","Objective 7",["Import policy controls routes coming into the routing table, not what OSPF advertises to neighbors.","Preference decides the active route between sources; it does not cause OSPF to advertise a route.","OSPF runs on its own and does not need BGP to advertise routes.",null]],["jj84",0,7,"A route matches no term in any policy in a chain. What decides its fate?",["It is always rejected","It is accepted with its original attributes","The protocol's default policy","The first term again"],2,"If no policy terminates the evaluation, the default policy of the protocol applies. It is not simply rejected.","Objective 7",["Routes are not always rejected; for example, the BGP default policy accepts active BGP routes.","Routes are not automatically accepted either; the protocol's default policy may reject them.",null,"Evaluation never loops back to the first term; it continues to the next policy or the default policy."]],["jj85",0,7,"Which route-filter match type matches 10.0.0.0/8 and all more specific routes?",["prefix-length-range","orlonger","longer","upto /8"],1,"`orlonger` includes the prefix and all more specifics. `longer` excludes the /8 itself.","Objective 7",["prefix-length-range matches only a given range of lengths and does not include 10.0.0.0/8 plus all more specifics by itself.",null,"`longer` matches only more-specific prefixes and excludes the /8 itself.","`upto /8` would only match the /8 itself, since the upper limit equals the prefix length."]],["jj86",0,7,"Which route-filter matches 192.168.0.0/16 and more specific prefixes only up to /24?",["192.168.0.0/16 upto /24","192.168.0.0/16 longer","192.168.0.0/24 exact","192.168.0.0/16 orlonger"],0,"`upto /24` limits matches to lengths /16 through /24. `orlonger` would also match /25 through /32.","Objective 7",[null,"`longer` matches /17 to /32 but excludes the /16 itself and has no /24 upper limit.","`192.168.0.0/24 exact` matches only that single /24, not the /16 or other /17-/24 routes.","`orlonger` matches every length up to /32, including /25 and longer."]],["jj87",0,7,"What happens to a packet that does not match any term in a Junos firewall filter?",["It is accepted","It is sent to the RE","It is counted and accepted","It is discarded"],3,"Every firewall filter ends with an implicit discard. A final accept term is needed to allow other traffic.","Objective 7",["Junos filters do not accept unmatched traffic; an explicit accept term is needed for that.","Unmatched traffic is not sent to the RE; it is dropped at the implicit end of the filter.","Nothing is counted for unmatched packets unless a term with a count action matched.",null]],["jj88",0,7,"Which firewall filter action drops a packet and sends an ICMP message back?",["discard","count","reject","next term"],2,"reject drops and sends ICMP (by default port unreachable). discard drops silently.","Objective 7",["discard drops the packet silently, with no ICMP message.","count is a non-terminating action that only increments a counter.",null,"next term continues evaluation; it does not drop or send ICMP."]],["jj89",0,7,"A filter term has `then count web-hits` and no other action. What happens to a matching packet?",["It is discarded","It is counted, then accepted","It moves on to the next filter in the list","It is rejected with ICMP"],1,"When a term has only non-terminating actions, Junos adds an implicit accept. It does not continue to the next filter.","Objective 7",["A count-only term does not discard; Junos adds an implicit accept.",null,"A term match ends evaluation; Junos does not move on to another filter after a terminating implicit accept.","Nothing tells Junos to reject; reject must be configured explicitly."]],["jj90",0,7,"An engineer wants to check whether prefix 172.20.5.0/24 is accepted by policy EXPORT-ISP before applying it. Which command?",["test policy EXPORT-ISP 172.20.5.0/24","show policy EXPORT-ISP 172.20.5.0/24 detail","commit check EXPORT-ISP","show route export EXPORT-ISP"],0,"`test policy` runs routing table entries through the policy and shows which match. `show policy` only displays the policy.","Objective 7",[null,"`show policy` displays the policy's configuration; it does not test which routes it accepts.","`commit check` validates the whole candidate configuration and takes no policy name.","`show route export` is not the command that tests a policy against a prefix."]],["jj91",0,7,"A router should drop packets whose source address is not reachable via the interface they arrived on. Which feature does this?",["A route-filter","An export policy on the interface","A discard static route","Unicast RPF strict mode"],3,"Strict uRPF checks that the best route back to the source uses the receiving interface. A route-filter works on routes, not packets.","Objective 7",["A route-filter is a routing policy match condition on routes, not a per-packet source check.","Export policies control which routes are advertised; they are not applied to interfaces to check packets.","A discard static route drops traffic sent to a destination, not traffic from a bad source.",null]],["jj92",0,7,"A routing policy term ends with `then { metric 10; }` and no accept or reject. What happens to a matching route?",["It is rejected immediately","It is accepted without changes","Its metric is set, then evaluation continues","The whole policy chain stops"],2,"A modifier such as metric is not terminating, so evaluation continues to the next term, next policy or the default policy. It is not accepted until a terminating action or default policy says so.","Objective 7",["metric is a non-terminating action, so the route is not rejected at this point.","The route is changed (its metric is set), and it is not accepted until a later terminating action or the default policy.",null,"Only a terminating action (accept or reject) stops evaluation; a modifier does not end the chain."]]]);
